  "notes": "<div style=\"background: linear-gradient(135deg, #3b0764 0%, #5b21b6 100%); border: 1px solid #a78bfa; border-radius: 14px; padding: 22px 24px; margin-bottom: 28px;\">\n<h2 style=\"color: #ddd6fe; font-size: 22px; margin: 0 0 8px; font-weight: 700;\">📖 Before We Begin — Story-Time Words</h2>\n<p style=\"color: #ddd6fe; font-size: 13px; margin: 0 0 16px; line-height: 1.6;\">My little friend, before we explore families and communities, let us learn some special words. Imagine we are sitting in a warm home, and I am telling you these words one by one. Ready? Let's begin!</p>\n<div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.5); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 18px; margin-bottom: 12px;\"><h3 style=\"color: #c4b5fd; font-size: 16px; margin: 0 0 8px;\">🏠 1. Family</h3><p style=\"color: #e9d5ff; font-size: 13px; line-height: 1.6; margin: 0;\">A family is the fundamental and most ancient unit of any society. It is a group of people who live together and care for each other. There are two main types: joint families (grandparents, parents, uncles, aunts, cousins) and nuclear families (parents and children only).</p></div>\n<div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.5); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 18px; margin-bottom: 12px;\"><h3 style=\"color: #c4b5fd; font-size: 16px; margin: 0 0 8px;\">👪 2. Joint Family</h3><p style=\"color: #e9d5ff; font-size: 13px; line-height: 1.6; margin: 0;\">A joint family has several generations living together — grandparents, parents, uncles and aunts, brothers, sisters and cousins. Everyone shares everything. In Indian languages, there is often no word for 'cousin' — cousins are just 'brothers' and 'sisters'!</p></div>\n<div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.5); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 18px; margin-bottom: 12px;\"><h3 style=\"color: #c4b5fd; font-size: 16px; margin: 0 0 8px;\">🏢 3. Nuclear Family</h3><p style=\"color: #e9d5ff; font-size: 13px; line-height: 1.6; margin: 0;\">A nuclear family is limited to a couple and their children, and sometimes one parent and children. It is a smaller, more intimate unit. Many couples in modern cities choose nuclear families because of jobs and space.</p></div>\n<div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.5); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 18px; margin-bottom: 12px;\"><h3 style=\"color: #c4b5fd; font-size: 16px; margin: 0 0 8px;\">👥 4. Community</h3><p style=\"color: #e9d5ff; font-size: 13px; line-height: 1.6; margin: 0;\">A community is a group of connected people who come together for various reasons — celebrating festivals, organising feasts, supporting each other. Communities can be based on religion, region, profession, or shared interests. The list is endless!</p></div>\n<div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.5); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 18px; margin-bottom: 12px;\"><h3 style=\"color: #c4b5fd; font-size: 16px; margin: 0 0 8px;\">🤝 5. Cooperation</h3><p style=\"color: #e9d5ff; font-size: 13px; line-height: 1.6; margin: 0;\">Cooperation means 'working together'. Each member of the family and community has a role and responsibility towards others. When everyone cooperates, the household and community run smoothly.</p></div>\n<div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.5); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 18px; margin-bottom: 12px;\"><h3 style=\"color: #c4b5fd; font-size: 16px; margin: 0 0 8px;\">⚖️ 6. Dharma</h3><p style=\"color: #e9d5ff; font-size: 13px; line-height: 1.6; margin: 0;\">Dharma means 'duty' or 'righteous conduct'. Following our dharma has been an important principle of Indian culture. The family is a 'school' where children learn dharma — their duty towards others.</p></div>\n<div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.5); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 18px; margin-bottom: 12px;\"><h3 style=\"color: #c4b5fd; font-size: 16px; margin: 0 0 8px;\">🙏 7. Ahimsa</h3><p style=\"color: #e9d5ff; font-size: 13px; line-height: 1.6; margin: 0;\">Ahimsa means 'non-violence' — not hurting anyone by thought, word, or deed. It is one of the important values children learn in the family. Mahatma Gandhi made ahimsa the foundation of India's freedom struggle.</p></div>\n<div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.5); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 18px; margin-bottom: 12px;\"><h3 style=\"color: #c4b5fd; font-size: 16px; margin: 0 0 8px;\">💸 8. Dana</h3><p style=\"color: #e9d5ff; font-size: 13px; line-height: 1.6; margin: 0;\">Dana means 'giving' — sharing what we have with others who need it more. It is an important value learned in the family. Giving can be of money, food, time, or knowledge.</p></div>\n<div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.5); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 18px; margin-bottom: 12px;\"><h3 style=\"color: #c4b5fd; font-size: 16px; margin: 0 0 8px;\">🧑‍🌾 9. Seva</h3><p style=\"color: #e9d5ff; font-size: 13px; line-height: 1.6; margin: 0;\">Seva means 'service' — doing things for others without expecting anything in return. Communities come together in seva during crises, like the Bhil community planting trees or volunteers feeding people during the Chennai floods.</p></div>\n<div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.5); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 18px; margin-bottom: 12px;\"><h3 style=\"color: #c4b5fd; font-size: 16px; margin: 0 0 8px;\">💜 10. Tyaga</h3><p style=\"color: #e9d5ff; font-size: 13px; line-height: 1.6; margin: 0;\">Tyaga means 'sacrifice' — giving up our own needs to take care of the family's or community's needs. In the story of Shalini, she gave up her silk dress so that her cousins could also get new clothes for Onam. That is tyaga.</p></div>\n</div>\n\n<h2>📝 Key Notes</h2>\n<h3>Types of Families</h3>\n<table class=\"styled-table\">\n<tr><th>Type</th><th>Members</th><th>Example</th></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Joint Family</strong></td><td>Grandparents, parents, uncles, aunts, cousins</td><td>Shalini's family in Kerala</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Nuclear Family</strong></td><td>Parents and children only</td><td>Common in modern cities</td></tr>\n</table>\n<h3>Values Learned in the Family</h3>\n<table class=\"styled-table\">\n<tr><th>Value</th><th>Sanskrit Word</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Non-violence</td><td><strong>Ahimsa</strong></td><td>Not hurting anyone by thought, word, or deed</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Giving</td><td><strong>Dana</strong></td><td>Sharing what we have with others</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Service</td><td><strong>Seva</strong></td><td>Doing things for others without expecting return</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Sacrifice</td><td><strong>Tyaga</strong></td><td>Giving up our own needs for the family/community</td></tr>\n</table>\n<h3>Real-Life Community Stories</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Bhil community (Jhabua, MP):</strong> Planted thousands of trees and dug trenches to conserve rainwater — the halma tradition of serving Mother Earth</li>\n<li><strong>Chennai floods (2015):</strong> Spiritual and religious organisations cooked and distributed food to stranded people</li>\n<li><strong>Kamal Parmar (Ahmedabad):</strong> Taught 150 underprivileged children for free, 5:30–9:30 pm every day, with free dinner</li>\n</ul>",
  "practice": "<h2>✏️ Practice</h2>\n<div class=\"practice-card\">\n<h3>🧠 Quick Recall</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>The family is the fundamental and most ___ unit of any society.</li>\n<li>A ___ family has several generations living together.</li>\n<li>A ___ family is limited to parents and children.</li>\n<li>'___' means 'working together'.</li>\n<li>The four values learned in the family are ___ (non-violence), ___ (giving), ___ (service), and ___ (sacrifice).</li>\n<li>A group of connected people is called a ___.</li>\n<li>The Bhil community planted thousands of trees following their ___ tradition.</li>\n<li>Kamal Parmar taught ___ underprivileged children for free in Ahmedabad.</li>\n</ol>\n<button class=\"reveal-btn\">Reveal Answers</button>\n<div class=\"reveal-answer\">\n<ol>\n<li>ancient</li>\n<li>joint</li>\n<li>nuclear</li>\n<li>Cooperation</li>\n<li>ahimsa, dana, seva, tyaga</li>\n<li>community</li>\n<li>halma</li>\n<li>150</li>\n</ol>\n</div>\n</div>",
